# @mirror/lens/cli/kintsugi — the coherence-settling loop as a stage.
#
# Second of eight verb sub-stages forward-promised in
# docs/specs/cli-as-prism.md §3 (table at §3 + §3.1 shape sketch).
# Realizes the `command kintsugi` declaration in mirror.spec at the
# stage altitude — the substrate level where the named loop
# (focus -> project -> shift -> settle) drives one tournament round-trip.
#
# === Why kintsugi is a stage, not a resonator ===
#
# Per cli-as-prism §2.1: the TOURNAMENT itself is structurally a
# `resonator` per optical-keywords.md §1.5 — a closed loop with output
# coupler — but the CLI sub-stage that drives one iteration is a `stage`
# rather than a `resonator`. Each invocation is a chain through the five
# ops, not a self-referential round-trip. The resonator lives at the
# kintsugi-engine altitude; the CLI surface is the stage that fires one
# round-trip from outside.
#
# Per cli-as-prism §5.2 (where the constraint sang): the five operations
# map onto the tournament loop WITHOUT FORCING. `kintsugi.settle` IS one
# iteration. `kintsugi.focus` IS peeking the next move. `kintsugi.split`
# IS enumerating candidate fills. The tournament was already the same
# algebra at a smaller scale; the recursion just made the algebra
# visible at the shell.
#
# === Family placement ===
#
# Parent bench: @mirror/lens/cli (shards/mirror/lens/cli.mirror).
# Family root:  @mirror/lens    (shards/mirror/lens.mirror).
# Sibling stages:
#   @mirror/lens/cli/compile    — landed at 737330f (the build pipeline)
#   @mirror/lens/cli/shatter    — materialize AST -> text (next)
#   @mirror/lens/cli/bootstrap  — autopoietic phase machine (next)
#   @mirror/lens/cli/join       — peer-entry (Glanville's verb)
#   @mirror/lens/cli/reflect    — VSM System 5 view
#   @mirror/lens/cli/time       — Elm-style time travel
#   @mirror/lens/cli/crack      — third-state lifecycle
#
# Path-namespace property: this file at
# `shards/mirror/lens/cli/kintsugi.mirror` declares
# `@mirror/lens/cli/kintsugi` and only that.
#
# Spec context: docs/specs/cli-as-prism.md §3, §3.1 (shape sketch is
#               this stage's sketch literally),
#               §5.2 (where kintsugi sang),
#               §7 (default-op rule: kintsugi declares `default focus`),
#               docs/specs/optical-keywords.md §1.2 (stage),
#               §1.5 (resonator — the tournament engine altitude),
#               mirror.spec (`command kintsugi { arg spec: ~f =
#               ~f'./mirror.spec'; flag target: list(str) = [];
#               flag emit_shatter: bool = false }`).
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-kintsugi-variety-io]] (Ashby
#                      requisite-variety; @fate tournament IS variety-
#                      maintenance — this stage drives one round),
#                      [[architecture-kintsugi-bias-lift]] (kintsugi
#                      bias = lift @facet -> @mirror; substrate-pull as
#                      gradient),
#                      [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]]
#                      (stage IS a sub-prism specialization),
#                      [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]]
#                      (file path matches namespace),
#                      [[architecture-lift-as-load-bearing]] (shift =
#                      basis transformation on the loss landscape).
# Related shards: lens/cli.mirror (parent bench, the produced-prism
#                 form the user types),
#                 lens/cli/compile.mirror (sibling — the prior
#                 sub-stage; same template, different operand naming),
#                 glass.mirror (imperfect / transparency / au carriers).
#
# === Composition with @song/movement (2026-07-06; SECOND WITNESS) ===
#
# `mirror kintsugi @spec` IS `@song/movement.close` at cli altitude —
# the closure event of the temporal-bounded epoch that IS the spec-
# construction game. Per shards/song/movement.mirror §3 (`close` — the
# movement's frame-closure event): close consumes the family-root's
# `song_settles` composed bilateral verdict at species altitude, decides
# StageFreight's publish + narrate cascade dispatch (publish + narrate
# on Splinter-pole; withhold on Narcissus-pole), emits the closed
# movement ref. When a human types `mirror kintsugi @spec` at the cli
# surface they discharge exactly this frame-closure action: the bounded
# frame that spawn opened (M2 TICK 1 `63ea934`; `mirror spawn` IS
# `@song/movement.enter`) closes here through the tournament iteration
# that ratifies the spec's `settle_on` conditions.
#
# The cli verb `mirror kintsugi @spec` IS the species-altitude action
# `close` at cli altitude. Same operation, two altitudes. Frame-close
# discipline: `spawn` opens the frame (enter); `kintsugi @spec` closes
# it (close). Together the two verbs make the movement — the bounded
# epoch's open-close discipline is exactly what the pair of cli verbs
# discharges at the surface.
#
# Structural claim (not analogy): `mirror kintsugi @spec` doesn't
# RESEMBLE `@song/movement.close`; it IS it, at cli altitude. The
# closure event of the temporal-bounded spec-construction epoch is
# structurally the same operation the species declares at species
# altitude; the cli surface is the altitude at which a human discharges
# it. The bounded-frame-shift discipline (per movement §"Bounded frame-
# shift" + Bateson Level III on-frame frame-flexibility) lifts to cli
# altitude without dilution: the kintsugi verb IS the frame-shift ON
# the frame, discharged at the shell.
#
# === Composition with @mirror/peer/beam (kintsugi → beam → @song) ===
#
# Composition chain at the M-cascade wire boundary:
#
#   MCP session (M1 `01443b3`) accumulates @spec via mq queries
#     ↓
#   `mirror kintsugi @spec` (this stage; M2 TICK 2) fires tournament;
#   `settle` action ratifies @spec's `settle_on` conditions
#     ↓
#   Ratified @spec OID passes to `@mirror/peer/beam.beam` (M2 TICK 1
#   `63ea934`; return-type upgrade runtime → @song)
#     ↓
#   `spawn` returns @song — the running peer's time-evolution operator
#
# The kintsugi.settle action feeds a ratified @spec OID into
# @mirror/peer/beam.beam which returns @song. The @song emerges at the
# spawn boundary post-tournament, NOT at kintsugi's return boundary —
# kintsugi's actions keep their `imperfect` return type (they may
# partial per recognition #306); the typed temporal frame surfaces at
# the spawn action after the tournament's variety-maintenance discharge
# ratifies the spec.
#
# === @spec provenance from MCP session (M1 → M2 wiring) ===
#
# The @spec that kintsugi settles IS accumulated by the MCP session
# gen_prism (M1 TICK 1 LANDED at `8eac1de`; canonical
# `@spectral/gen_prism/mcp_session`) across mq queries. Provenance
# chain:
#
#   mq queries (LSP-shaped MCP surface across the session)
#     ↓
#   session state (crystals in @mirror/store — the Apache-2.0 floor;
#   #43 content-addressed build system; M6 TICK 1 `884f433`)
#     ↓
#   @spec OID at ratification (session state settles into a byte-
#   addressable ref carrying the accumulated query trajectory)
#     ↓
#   kintsugi.settle (this stage; one tournament iteration ratifies
#   `settle_on` conditions per the @fate variety-maintenance contract)
#     ↓
#   @mirror/peer/beam (M2 TICK 1) consumes the ratified @spec OID
#     ↓
#   @song (post-tournament typed temporal frame at the peer's
#   trajectory altitude)
#
# The @spec is not authored ex nihilo at kintsugi's boundary; it is
# the accumulated composition of an mq query trajectory that lives in
# the MCP session gen_prism's state as crystals in @mirror/store. When
# `settle_on` ratifies, this stage's settle action fires one tournament
# iteration; when the tournament converges cleanly, the ratified OID
# feeds spawn and @song emerges as the running peer's time-evolution
# operator.
#
# === Citations (M-cascade wiring + collapse spec) ===
#
# - Collapse spec `docs/specs/mcp-spec-song-collapse.md` §5, §10 (Mara
#   2cfd2a7): the MCP session → @spec construction → kintsugi @spec
#   ratification → @mirror/peer/beam → @song wire. This shard IS the
#   kintsugi-@spec verb dispatch at cli altitude. Composes with #S2
#   (shift-at-temporal, LANDED), #S3 (five-op temporal specialisation,
#   LANDED at Seam Phase D `10c34cf`), #S4 (cascade-shape altitude-
#   portable, LANDED).
#
# - @spectral/gen_prism/mcp_session (M1 TICK 1 LANDED at `8eac1de`;
#   Seam Phase D RATIFY; "MCP session IS gen_prism" PROMOTED): sibling
#   substrate. The MCP session gen_prism IS the state machine that
#   accumulates @spec across mq queries; this stage IS the substrate
#   that consumes the ratified @spec via `settle`. Together with
#   @mirror/peer/beam (M2 TICK 1 LANDED at `63ea934`) they close the mq
#   → @spec → @song wiring: M1 accumulates the spec, M2 TICK 2 (this
#   stage) ratifies the spec through the tournament, M2 TICK 1 spawns
#   the peer whose trajectory IS the spec's time-evolution.
#
# - #43 (mirror IS content-addressed build system; LANDED at M6 TICK 1
#   `884f433`; empirically substrate-fact per M1 TICK 1 `8eac1de` +
#   M2 TICK 1 `63ea934`): kintsugi IS the fourth first-order consumer
#   of the Apache-2.0 floor. The chain: M6 self-declaration → M1
#   mcp_session → M2 TICK 1 spawn → M2 TICK 2 kintsugi. The
#   tournament's settled crystals are OIDs in @mirror/store; the
#   ratified @spec OID that kintsugi.settle emits IS a content-
#   addressed ref that spawn consumes. Recognition #43's operational
#   grounding runs THROUGH this stage: the CAS-advance from
#   accumulated session state to ratified @spec is where the content-
#   addressed build system's discipline earns the M-cascade's
#   composition.
#
# === Candidate recognition: cli-verbs ARE species-altitude actions ===
#
# Second-witness landing per M2 TICK 1 Seam Phase D `b50fedc` §3.
# First witness: `mirror spawn` IS `@song/movement.enter` at cli
# altitude (M2 TICK 1 `63ea934`; shards/mirror/peer/beam.mirror (renamed 2026-07-08 Tick 2)
# "Composition with @song/movement" section). This tick lands the
# second witness: `mirror kintsugi @spec` IS `@song/movement.close`
# at cli altitude.
#
# Two witnesses land the candidate. `spawn` opens the frame (enter);
# `kintsugi @spec` closes it (close). Together they specialise
# @song/movement's two actions (enter/close) at cli altitude — same
# operation, different altitudes.
#
# Structural claim: some cli verbs are species-altitude actions
# PROJECTED to the cli surface. Not analogy — identity by structural
# specialisation. The cli altitude is not a metaphor over the species
# altitude; it is the projection at which a human discharges the
# species-altitude action through the shell. Distinct from #S3 (five-
# op temporal specialisation; LANDED) and #S4 (cascade-shape altitude-
# portable; LANDED). Fresh candidate; second witness lands here; if
# both witnesses discharge cleanly at Seam Phase D, the candidate
# promotes CANDIDATE → LANDED.
#
# === Return-type transition (2026-07-06 / post-@song) ===
#
# Original 2026-06-12 landing: kintsugi actions return
# `imperfect(settled-graph-ref)`. Arc 6 close (`eb50a61`) landed the
# @song family + 5 species (movement, voice, progression, narrative,
# phrase); M2 TICK 1 (`63ea934`) upgraded @mirror/peer/beam (fmr @mirror/spawn) to return
# @song. This tick (M2 TICK 2) names the composition: when kintsugi
# settles an @spec whose `settle_on` ratifies, the ratified OID feeds
# @mirror/peer/beam.beam which returns @song. The kintsugi actions
# themselves keep their `imperfect` return type (they may partial per
# recognition #306); the @song emerges at the spawn boundary post-
# tournament, not at kintsugi's boundary. The typed temporal frame
# surfaces AFTER the tournament ratifies — kintsugi is the ratifier,
# spawn is the frame-opener whose typed return names the post-
# ratification state.
#
# This is substrate-honest witness that the @song wire post-dates the
# shard's 2026-06-12 landing: pre-@song, kintsugi's contribution to
# the pipeline was a settled-graph ref with no downstream typed frame;
# post-@song, the ratified @spec OID feeds a spawn action whose
# @song return names the peer's temporal-evolution trajectory. The
# shard's return type does NOT change; what changes is that its
# downstream composition (via the @mirror/peer/beam wire) now surfaces
# @song at the M-cascade boundary.

stage @mirror/lens/cli/kintsugi {
  # Per cli-as-prism §7: kintsugi declares `default focus` — the
  # standard read-default. Bare `mirror kintsugi` peeks the next
  # tournament move (no write). Settling a tournament iteration is a
  # real substrate write per the @fate variety-maintenance contract;
  # the user pays only for the explicit `settle`. Reading is free.
  default focus

  # The five operations the produced prism exposes on the kintsugi
  # sub-manifold (the tournament's working set). Each operand below
  # names the natural shape of the manifold's element at that op's
  # slot, per the spec §3.1 sketch convention and §2.1's per-verb
  # decisions:
  #   focus    target       — peek the next tournament candidate (no write)
  #   project  predicate    — filter the working set by what to repair
  #                          (target, eigenvalue range, transparency band)
  #   split    candidate    — enumerate candidate fills
  #   shift    basis        — basis-transform on the loss landscape,
  #                          per [[architecture-lift-as-load-bearing]]
  #   settle   iteration    — the ONE write; run one tournament iteration
  #                          AND emit the proof block recording the
  #                          variety-maintenance act
  focus    target
  project  predicate
  split    candidate
  shift    basis
  settle   iteration
}

# === Kintsugi-specific actions ===
#
# Realizes mirror.spec's `command kintsugi { arg spec: ~f =
# ~f'./mirror.spec'; flag target: list(str) = []; flag emit_shatter:
# bool = false }` at the stage altitude. The action signature carries
# the same shape — a spec-file ref, an optional target whitelist, and
# the emit-shatter flag — at the substrate altitude so the dispatcher
# can walk from cli surface to settled call without leaving the
# substrate. The Rust runtime walks these declarations; it does not
# name commands. Substrate-pull discipline per
# [[architecture-fragmentation-is-the-rust-substrate]].
#
# Honest `partials` from birth (per recognition #306): the return type
# is the substrate's `imperfect` functor (declared in
# shards/glass.mirror as `imperfect(a, e, l) = success(a) |
# partial(a, l) | failure(e, l)`). A tournament round that converges
# fully returns success; a round that lifts loss without fully closing
# the gap returns partial-with-loss carrying the transparency-tagged
# residual opacity, so the kintsugi loop's next round (or a higher
# altitude) can read the unresolved fracture. Per
# [[feedback-manual-closure-is-training-pull]]: manual patching with
# opaque carriers breaks the discriminator — partial-with-loss is the
# substrate-aligned shape.

# kintsugi(spec) — run one tournament iteration against the spec at
# `spec` (defaults to `./mirror.spec` per mirror.spec's command
# declaration). Returns the imperfect carrying the settled-graph ref
# on success, the partial carrying the transparency-tagged loss on
# tolerable variety-shortfall, the failure with reason on hard error.
kintsugi(spec: ref) -> imperfect { \ }

# kintsugi_targeted(spec, target) — same as kintsugi but with an
# explicit target whitelist (matches mirror.spec's `flag target:
# list(str) = []`). When `target` is non-empty, the tournament's
# working set is restricted to the named targets (the
# variety-maintenance act runs over a sub-manifold); empty list means
# run over the spec's full settle_on set.
kintsugi_targeted(spec: ref, target: list) -> imperfect { \ }

# kintsugi_with_shatter(spec, target, emit_shatter) — full-shape entry
# matching mirror.spec's three-flag declaration. When `emit_shatter`
# is true, the iteration also writes a `.shatter` projection of the
# settled subgraph (per @mirror/lens/cli/shatter contract). This is
# the cross-stage seam: kintsugi's settle composes with shatter's
# settle when the flag is set; the two stages share the @mirror/store
# write boundary.
kintsugi_with_shatter(spec: ref, target: list, emit_shatter: bool) -> imperfect { \ }

out @mirror/lens/cli/kintsugi
out kintsugi
out kintsugi_targeted
out kintsugi_with_shatter
